<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leiria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leiria is a city in central Portugal, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> took it from the Moors and built its castle in 1135, and it became a royal residence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> met there in 1254, the first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> with town representatives, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-rapontchombo">King Denis</a> planted the nearby Pinhal de Leiria pine forest. It is now the capital of Leiria District.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leiria.jpg" alt="Leiria" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jocelyn Erskine-Kellie from London, UK · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leiria_(25746536508)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
